{"explanation":"Rule: Rage adds its damage bonus only 'when you make a melee weapon Attack using Strength.' The thrown property says throwing a thrown weapon is 'to make a ranged attack', and Ranged Attacks lists 'hurl a handaxe' as a ranged attack. Fact: you throw a handaxe while raging, using Strength. That is a ranged attack made with Strength. Conclusion: on the most direct reading it is not a melee weapon attack, so the rage bonus does not apply.

The ruling turns on the phrase 'melee weapon Attack', and the text allows two readings. (1) It means a melee attack made with a weapon. The Melee Attacks entry uses 'melee weapon attack' for attacks against foes within reach, set against unarmed strikes. A thrown handaxe makes a ranged attack, so no bonus. (2) It means an attack made with a melee weapon. The handaxe is a 'Simple melee weapon' and stays one when thrown: the thrown entry says 'if the weapon is a melee weapon, you use the same ability modifier.' The Strength entry also counts thrown javelins as 'attacking with a melee weapon'. On this reading, a Strength-based throw qualifies and you would add the bonus.

The entries never define 'melee weapon attack', so they do not settle which reading is meant. The more direct reading, which follows the explicit 'ranged attack' language, gives no.","ambiguous":true,"answer":{"kind":"yes-or-no","verdict":"no"},"cites":["class-features > barbarian > rage","weapon-properties > thrown","making-an-attack > ranged-attacks","making-an-attack > melee-attacks","equipment > handaxe","using-each-ability > strength > attack-rolls-and-damage"]}
